	'how-we-work_c1_h' => 'How does our staffing rule determine kitchen team size?',
	'how-we-work_c1_b' => 'Every lunchroom contract operates on our dedicated staffing rule of one team member per forty lunches served. This ratio ensures that kitchen staff never work under unmanageable pressure during peak lunch service and that each plate receives focused attention. When a dining room serves eighty lunches, two trained team members are assigned to the kitchen. For a standard workplace serving two hundred lunches, five dedicated staff members manage the preparation, hot pass, counter service, and dining room floor. This consistent ratio maintains reliable service timing across small, standard, and large facilities alike.',
	'how-we-work_c2_h' => 'What happens throughout a standard service day?',
	'how-we-work_c2_b' => 'Each service day follows four consistent operational stages designed to deliver punctual lunch service. Kitchen prep begins promptly at 06:30 as fresh ingredients arrive and stocks are started for the midday meal. By 11:15, all warm dishes and sides are completed and plated onto the hot pass for inspection. The dining floor opens to workplace staff at 11:30, maintaining continuous service throughout the midday window until 13:30. Kitchen close-down and sanitation finish by 14:30, leaving cooking equipment, counters, and dining tables fully cleaned and sanitized for the following morning.',
	'how-we-work_c3_h' => 'What are the four primary roles on each kitchen team?',
	'how-we-work_c3_b' => 'On-site lunchroom operations depend on four distinct roles working in coordination throughout the day. The prep cook handles early morning ingredient preparation, vegetable cutting, and stock simmering starting at 06:30. The pass cook manages hot cooking stations, protein roasting, and tray assembly to ensure every meal meets service standards by 11:15. The dining floor server manages counter service, welcomes workplace diners, and maintains a clean dining environment between 11:30 and 13:30. Finally, the sanitation and close lead oversees hot water washing, kitchen hygiene logs, and workstation sanitizing through final close at 14:30.',
	'how-we-work_c4_h' => 'How are food allergens and dietary requirements managed?',
	'how-we-work_c4_b' => 'Allergen management follows strict kitchen protocols to safeguard workplace diners with food sensitivities. Preparation benches, cutting boards, and cookware are color-coded and kept separate for distinct ingredient categories. Every daily dish displayed on the counter is accompanied by clear allergen labeling that identifies standard allergens, including dairy, eggs, fish, gluten, nuts, and soy. Kitchen team members review daily recipe specification sheets before morning prep begins, ensuring complete transparency for diners who require specific dietary accommodations. Our food safety lead establishes these handling procedures across all workplace sites to maintain unwavering standards.',
