	'menus_c1_h' => 'How is our four-week menu cycle planned and rotated?',
	'menus_c1_b' => 'Our menus operate on a predictable four-week rotation cycle that prevents menu fatigue while simplifying inventory purchasing and kitchen preparation. Executive chef Tomás Ferreira and dietitian Amara Nwosu collaborate to develop balanced menus that provide wholesome midday fuel for diverse workforces. Every day features two distinct hot main courses alongside one dedicated plant-based main, ensuring satisfying options for all dining preferences. Dishes rotate through week one, week two, week three, and week four before refreshing, allowing workplace teams to look forward to their favorite meals while continually enjoying balanced nutritional variety.',
	'menus_c2_h' => 'What dishes appear during a sample service week?',
	'menus_c2_b' => 'A sample service week illustrates how our kitchen delivers variety across each weekday service window. Monday features roasted chicken with steamed greens alongside baked fish and a lentil vegetable stew. Tuesday presents braised beef with root vegetables, seared salmon, and a spiced chickpea bake. Wednesday offers herb-crusted pork loin, baked cod with dill, and roasted eggplant with toasted sesame grains. Thursday brings grilled chicken thighs, steamed white fish, and a savory tofu and vegetable stir-fry. Friday concludes with slow-cooked beef stew, roasted fish fillets, and a hearty white bean and vegetable pot.',
	'menus_c3_h' => 'What principles guide our dietitian\'s nutritional standards?',
	'menus_c3_b' => 'Dietitian Amara Nwosu reviews every recipe in our four-week cycle to verify nutritional balance across carbohydrate, protein, and vegetable ratios. Workplace lunches are formulated to sustain steady energy throughout afternoon work hours without causing post-meal fatigue. Salt and refined sugars are carefully moderated, while unrefined grains, seasonal produce, and clean cooking techniques form the foundation of our cooking style. Amara conducts quarterly ingredient reviews alongside purchasing manager Daniel Roth, ensuring that kitchen staples meet consistent nutritional criteria and that cooking fats remain clean and wholesome across all sites.',
	'menus_c4_h' => 'How are allergen labels displayed at the service pass?',
	'menus_c4_b' => 'Every plate leaving the kitchen pass is accompanied by clear, standardized allergen labeling displayed prominently above the counter. Labels clearly indicate the presence of dairy, eggs, fish, shellfish, tree nuts, peanuts, wheat, and soy across all daily mains and side dishes. Recipe cards containing precise ingredient lists are maintained at the pass, allowing dining floor servers to answer ingredient inquiries promptly. Lucas Moreau, our food safety lead, conducts regular audits across all kitchen locations to verify that labeling matches prepared ingredients and that no unmonitored cross-contact occurs during service.',
