	'starting_title' => 'Starting a contract',
	'starting_s' => 'four quiet stages',
	'starting_l' => 'Launching an on-site lunchroom follows four predictable stages from initial assessment to ongoing daily service. We provide a transparent onboarding timeline so your organization knows precisely what to expect at every step.',
	'alt_m17_starting' => 'A smiling team of three stands behind the serving counter, ready before service.',
	'alt_m13_starting' => 'A wide, empty staff dining room in daylight before service, with tables set.',
	'starting_tab' => 'Starting',
	'starting_c1_h' => 'What are the four stages of beginning a lunchroom contract?',
	'starting_c1_b' => 'Our onboarding process unfolds across four structured milestones over an eight-week timeline. In Week 1, our operations team conducts a complimentary on-site visit to inspect your dining space, kitchen facilities, and utility points. During Weeks 2 and 3, executive chef Tomás Ferreira and client manager Priya Raman develop a customized menu rotation and staffing schedule based on our 1:40 ratio. Week 6 features a fully operational trial week, giving your workforce a direct experience of our meal service. In Week 8, permanent service commences with fully assigned kitchen personnel, opening the dining floor for official daily operations.',
	'starting_c2_h' => 'What information and facility access do we need from your team?',
	'starting_c2_b' => 'To prepare an accurate operational proposal, we require basic information regarding your workplace operations and physical facilities. We ask for your estimated daily dining counts, expected break schedules, and details regarding any existing kitchen equipment, power connections, water lines, and waste disposal areas. If your workplace requires multi-shift coverage or off-peak hot boxes, sharing shift timetables early helps our planning team align staff assignments. Providing floor plans and facility access during the Week 1 site visit ensures our technical team can confirm kitchen layout suitability before writing the operational plan.',
	'starting_c3_h' => 'How does the Week 6 trial week operate?',
	'starting_c3_b' => 'The Week 6 trial week is a complete, five-day live test of our lunchroom service offered at a flat fee of $2,400. Our kitchen crew arrives on-site to handle ingredient receiving, morning food preparation, hot pass service, and dining room sanitizing under actual operating conditions. Workplace employees sample our daily rotation of two hot mains and one plant-based dish, experiencing the quality and timing firsthand. This trial allows both management and staff to evaluate service efficiency, portion sizes, and queue management before making a long-term commitment to a full operational agreement.',
	'starting_c4_h' => 'What are the contract duration and notice terms?',
	'starting_c4_b' => 'Our standard commercial agreement requires a minimum term of twelve months following the completion of your trial week and initial onboarding. This twelve-month baseline provides operational stability, allowing us to recruit dedicated culinary personnel, invest in site-specific smallwares, and establish consistent supply arrangements with local producers through purchasing manager Daniel Roth. Either party may terminate or renegotiate the contract by providing ninety days written notice. This notice period ensures proper transition management, fair treatment for kitchen staff members, and orderly transfer or decommission of dining facilities without abrupt disruption.',
